final_answer:
An NBFC can declare a dividend only after meeting the minimum prudential requirements set out in the eligibility criteria: it must satisfy the applicable capital‑adequacy norms (CRAR, Tier I/II, leverage ratio, ANW) for each of the last three financial years (or since registration if younger), keep the net‑NPA ratio below 6 % in each of those years, and comply with Section 45IC of the RBI Act (or the sector‑specific provisions) [B.]. If an NBFC (other than an SPD) fails to meet these prudential requirements for the three years, it may still declare a dividend but only up to a 10 % payout ratio and only if it meets the capital‑adequacy requirement [C.].

When a dividend is declared, the payout must include dividends on both equity shares and compulsorily convertible preference shares that are part of Tier 1 capital/Owned Fund. Any extraordinary or exceptional profit, or any auditor‑qualified statement indicating profit overstatement, must be deducted from net profit before calculating the payout ratio [C.].

The maximum dividend payout ratio is capped as follows: 
- Type I NBFC (holding a Certificate of Registration) – no ceiling; 
- Core Investment Companies (CIC) – 60 %; 
- Small Finance NBFCs (SPD) – 60 %; 
- All other NBFCs – 50 % (except NBFC‑Base Layer entities with customer interface but not accepting public funds, which have no ceiling) [C.].
The Board must ensure that the total dividend proposed for the financial year does not exceed the applicable ceiling, and the RBI or NHB will not consider any ad‑hoc dispensation requests [C.].
Additionally, NBFC‑P2P entities must comply with the specific RBI directions on dividend declaration for peer‑to‑peer platforms [C.].
